{
  "schemaVersion": 4,
  "id": "t09-011",
  "track": "track-09",
  "title": "AWS architecture identity networking compute and data services",
  "status": "source-backed-draft-generation-enabled",
  "concepts": [
    "An AWS organization contains accounts that create administrative, security, and billing boundaries. IAM identities and temporary role sessions authorize API actions; cross-account access is an explicit trust and permission relationship.",
    "AWS Regions contain Availability Zones, while some services and edge features are global or regional in different ways. A VPC spans a Region and subnets are associated with individual Availability Zones.",
    "An IAM policy is evaluated in context: identity permissions, resource policies, trust policies, organization controls, permissions boundaries, and session policies can combine. A role is assumed to obtain temporary credentials rather than shared as a long-lived user password.",
    "A VPC route table determines destinations and targets. Security groups are stateful resource-level filters; network ACLs are stateless subnet-level filters. Public reachability also depends on routes, gateways, addresses, and service configuration.",
    "AWS compute choices range from EC2 instances to managed container orchestration, application platforms, and Lambda functions. Storage and database services likewise expose distinct durability, consistency, performance, and operating responsibilities.",
    "AWS Well-Architected reviews use six pillars: operational excellence, security, reliability, performance efficiency, cost optimization, and sustainability. Service-specific quotas, availability, and pricing must be checked for the selected Region."
  ],
