  "accuracyFacts": [
    {
      "fact": "SCPs limit the maximum permissions available to IAM principals in member accounts. RCPs restrict external principals accessing supported resources in member accounts. Neither grants permissions; both have service/account applicability limits, and RCPs do not affect resources in the Organizations management account.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_scps.html",
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/organizations/latest/userguide/orgs_manage_policies_rcps.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "Use Global Tables version 2019.11.21 (Current) for new designs. It supports MREC and MRSC; MREC is the default and asynchronously replicates, while MRSC synchronously replicates to another Region before success and provides strongly consistent reads across MRSC replicas. MRSC must use exactly three Regions (three replicas or two replicas plus a witness), has a restricted supported-Region set, does not support TTL or LSIs, and cannot add replicas or change mode after creation. Keep the legacy 2017.11.29 mode clearly identified as legacy and do not generalize its limitations to current global tables.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/HowItWorks.ReadConsistency.html",
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/V2globaltables_HowItWorks.html",
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/amazondynamodb/latest/developerguide/globaltables-CoreConcepts.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "AWS renamed the established Lambda programming model Lambda Event Functions when Lambda Web Functions launched on 2026-10-12. Lambda Web Functions are a separate direct-HTTP model: the application listens on a port, supports Node.js runtimes at launch, and uses immutable revisions plus endpoints. Do not describe a Lambda function URL as the new Web Functions model or imply Web Functions support every Lambda Event Function runtime.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/aws-lambda-web-functions/",
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/web-functions.html",
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/web-functions-how-it-works.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "As of the AWS announcement on 2026-11-19, on-demand Lambda Event Functions support up to 1,800 seconds (30 minutes) for synchronous, asynchronous, and event-source mapping invocations. On Lambda Managed Instances, synchronous invocations support 30 minutes and asynchronous/event-source mapping invocations support 5,400 seconds (90 minutes), except Amazon MQ and Amazon DocumentDB event source mappings remain at 15 minutes. Initialization remains limited to 10 seconds on-demand and 15 minutes on Managed Instances. Durable async executions can have a one-year total execution timeout, distinct from each invocation's function timeout. Do not generalize the 90-minute limit to all Lambda invocations or Web Functions.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/configuration-timeout.html",
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/invocation-sync.html",
        "https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/11/aws-lambda-30-minute-ondemand-event-functions/",
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/lambda-managed-instances-best-practices.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "AWS announced Lambda durable functions in 2025 and raised their maximum durable operations per execution to 30,000 and checkpoint payload to 1 MB on 2026-10-13. Those are service quotas, not unlimited workflow guarantees; retries, idempotency, payload persistence, regional quotas, and cost still matter.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://aws.amazon.com/about-aws/whats-new/2026/10/aws-lambda-durable-gen-limit-increase/",
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/lambda/latest/dg/gettingstarted-limits.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "AWS App Mesh support ended and it reached full shutdown on 2026-09-30 and should not be recommended for new designs as a current managed service. The service is outside this lesson's supplied inventory; when discussing older architectures, identify its shutdown and direct learners to current AWS service guidance rather than inventing a one-for-one successor.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/general/latest/gr/full_shutdown_services.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "fact": "AWS Well-Architected Framework was updated 2026-11-16: six pillar whitepapers consolidated into one framework, a Cloud Foundations section added for organization-wide platform capabilities, and workload pillars retained in Cloud Workloads. Cloud Foundations has seven capability areas; it is not an additional workload pillar.",
      "sourceUrls": [
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/framework.html",
        "https://docs.aws.amazon.com/wellarchitected/latest/framework/cloud-foundations.html"
      ]
    }
  ],
